A||||7||Formula 18||Minivan vs Camaro||||1||
Z||000000||Formula 18||09-13-2000||09:53 PM||||Hey all you 3.4L guys, can anyone help me out here. Lou Dias has been talking about these minivan engines and I was wonder what the differance is? Is it easier to swap the minivan because it is all redy set up transverse? Does that mean no starter switch?<BR>Is there a big HP Torque differance between the two? What about cams, heads, manifolds, ect. <P>Thanks in advance<BR><P>------------------<BR>Brian<BR> -88 Formula||||reg||1
Z||000001||Terrybogin||09-13-2000||10:24 PM||||The Fiero intake and electronics will not fit the minivan engine's Aluminum heads, but the starter location is correct. The Camaro engine will accept all the Fiero components, but will require the starter holes to be redrilled. If you are good with wiring and ECM's the minivan has an advantage- better cam and better flowing heads.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Sarlacc||09-13-2000||11:10 PM||||I think he was referring to the 96 Lumina Minivan, it came with the 3400 pushrod V6:<P>Horsepower   180 @ 5200 RPM<BR>Torque       205 @ 4000 RPM<P>You would not need to mess with moving the starter. You could even use the iron heads and fiero intake on this block if you change the pistons, but you would lose some of that extra 20 "stock" horsepower that the aluminum heads and intake from the 3400 produce.  Fierosound is doing this conversion with the iron heads, he even retained the roller cam by using custom length pushrods.  See this thread for more info:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/005508.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/005508.html</A> <P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||lou_dias||09-14-2000||12:37 PM||||Again, I would stick with the SFI and get/make a wiring kit.  Distributors suck!||||reg||1
Z||000004||88formula||09-14-2000||04:51 PM||||You would have more choices for camshafts with the Camaro engine than you would have with the minivan engine because the minivan engine uses a roller cam and the Camaro engine does not. If you wanted to change the minivan engine camshaft you would have to have one custom ground. With a healthy roller camshaft though the minivan engine would make more HP and torque than the Camaro engine with a flat tappet camshaft and the same fiero iron heads. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Sarlacc||09-14-2000||06:24 PM||||I would agree with Lou, since the 3400 has a crankshaft sensor it would be better to keep the DIS system even if one was to use the fiero computer.  The DIS control module will hook up quite easily to the fiero harness, the wire color codes are even the same.  I would not use the OBD-II computer. Even though SFI offers better gas milleage, it really isnt much of a performance advantage and it is just not worth it when you consider the complications and limitations inherent with the OBD-II system.  There are various things you lose by wiring in the Fiero MPFI on the 3400: the cold start system, fan switch, and EGR control.  You could easily tap the water neck for a fan switch.  But, I have no clue as to how one would setup any kind of a cold start injector on the 3400 intake, perhaps it is not even needed if you dont live in a extremely cold climate?  Would the Fiero EGR valve work on it?<BR>BTW. You can use a flat tappet cam on the 3400 - its not even a problem. But installing a roller cam in the camaro block would be a very expensive endeavor. ;-)<p>[This message has been edited by Sarlacc (edited 09-14-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||lou_dias||09-15-2000||12:35 PM||||Why are you worried about replacing the roller cam... It already offers 20 more hp than the camaro engine and if you want more lift/power, just replace the rocker arms with 1.6 ratio arms or higher.  I've got all sorts of performance parts in my camaro swap and I still don't have 180 gross hp...and worse gas mileage...  Then there's the porting of the intake and heads of the minivan engine as well as a better exhaust setup.  I believe it would fair very well against the TDOC 3.4 when all is said and done.<BR>Also, why let the Fiero computer run the 3.4?  You CAN reprogram OBD ][ for performance.  Just feed the Fiero's computer the basic signalls that it needs to know that the car is running and let the real computer take care of the rest.<BR>People seem to be too fixiated on having there engine say "Fiero" across the intake manifold.  If the Fiero were made today, it's engine would look like the minivan's.<BR>The Fiero's engine is 2 generations old now.  Let's get with the times and compete with everyone else on the same level.<p>[This message has been edited by lou_dias (edited 09-15-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||Sarlacc||09-15-2000||04:26 PM||||I would agree with everthing you said above Lou, except the thing about reprogramming the OBD-II system.  There really is not much of an interest out there in the complications involved with reprogramming these systems, at least not for the 60 degree engines.  I do know that this type of sevice is offered for the 3800sc in the Grand Prix's but it runs well over $300.  I am sure that if the demand grows for reprogramming the 3100 and 3400 P6 PCM's, that this service would eventually become readily available, but until then it would probably be more feasable to use an OBD-I system.  Since the Fiero system already uses OBD-I, it would be a good candidate for this, but perhaps there is an even better ECM (near plug-n-play) for an engine swap such as this?||||reg||1
